  "learningConceptName": "{id, select, access{Nearby places} tradeoffs{Trade-offs} noise_distance{Distance and noise} noise_shielding{Noise shielding} exposure{People's exposure} connectivity{Connected habitats} fragmentation{Fragmentation} maturity{Nature takes time} budget_loop{The town budget loop} delay{Delayed effects} feedback{Feedback loops} mixed_city{A balanced neighbourhood} resilience{Resilience} other{City systems}}",
  "@learningConceptName": {"placeholders": {"id": {"type": "String"}}},
  "learningConceptCause": "{id, select, access{Homes work better when everyday destinations and green space are close enough to reach.} tradeoffs{One decision can help one goal and make another harder. Good planning balances both.} noise_distance{Sound becomes quieter as it travels away from a road or another source.} noise_shielding{Buildings and vegetation between a source and a home can reduce some sound.} exposure{Noise only harms the quiet score where people actually live; both level and number of residents matter.} connectivity{Plants and animals benefit when suitable habitat forms a connected patch.} fragmentation{Roads and development split habitat into smaller, more disturbed pieces.} maturity{A newly planted forest cannot provide the full value of an old forest immediately.} budget_loop{Residents and jobs bring revenue; construction and maintenance spend it; the remaining budget limits later choices.} delay{Population, forests and finances respond over months or years rather than instantly.} feedback{A change can circle through the system and eventually strengthen or balance itself.} mixed_city{Homes, jobs, shops and green space work together when distances remain short.} resilience{A resilient city keeps working when conditions change and does not rely on one fragile solution.} other{Land use changes several connected parts of the city.}}",
  "@learningConceptCause": {"placeholders": {"id": {"type": "String"}}},
  "learningConceptModel": "{id, select, noise_distance{The simulation adds sound energy from each source and reduces point sources by 20 dB for every tenfold increase in distance. Road segments combine into line-like behaviour.} noise_shielding{The straight path from source to receiver is checked cell by cell. Forest, parks and dense buildings add simplified attenuation.} exposure{Each home's residents weight its local sound level. The indicator combines those resident-weighted results.} connectivity{Eight neighbouring directions join habitat cells into patches; effective mesh size represents connectivity.} fragmentation{Threat fields from roads and buildings reduce habitat quality with distance, while smaller patches reduce effective habitat.} maturity{Tile age moves habitat stock toward its potential with land-type-specific recovery times.} budget_loop{Monthly tax and job revenue is balanced against maintenance and construction costs. Population and filled jobs update the next month's revenue.} delay{The model advances in fixed monthly steps, with stocks moving only partway toward their potential each step.} access{Distance kernels and walking radii estimate how easily residents reach shops, work and green space.} tradeoffs{All indicators are calculated from the same map and stocks, so one placement can create several positive and negative deltas.} feedback{Attractiveness affects population; population affects traffic and revenue; traffic affects noise and air, which feed back into attractiveness.} mixed_city{A gravity model links residents to jobs, while walking radii estimate retail and green access.} resilience{Several indicators and the remaining budget are considered together instead of optimizing a single score.} other{The simulation combines spatial fields with stocks that change once per month.}}",
  "@learningConceptModel": {"placeholders": {"id": {"type": "String"}}},
  "learningConceptLaw": "{id, select, noise_distance{Based on the CNOSSOS-EU propagation method and ISO-style distance attenuation.} noise_shielding{CNOSSOS-EU includes ground, foliage and screening effects; Hectopolis uses a transparent simplification.} exposure{The 55 dB(A) daytime reference for homes comes from Germany's TA Lärm. This game is not a site assessment.} connectivity{Biotope values follow Germany's BKompV; habitat quality and threats follow the open InVEST approach.} fragmentation{The representation combines BKompV values, InVEST threats and the ecological species–area relationship.} maturity{Recovery times are educational estimates documented with the model parameters.} access{Green access uses the WHO 300 m recommendation; retail access uses an established Huff choice model.} budget_loop{Revenue magnitudes use official German municipal-finance statistics and simplified averages.} delay{Time constants are documented assumptions, not predictions for a particular real city.} tradeoffs{There is no single governing threshold: this represents the planning principle of weighing public interests.} feedback{The loop is a system-dynamics representation, not a legal rule.} mixed_city{Walking and mobility assumptions use BBSR accessibility research and Germany's MiD travel survey.} resilience{Resilience is represented as balanced goal performance, not as a statutory standard.} other{Every numerical parameter has a source in the model documentation.}}",
  "@learningConceptLaw": {"placeholders": {"id": {"type": "String"}}},
